  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Isolated afternoon showers and a couple \nthundershowers possible Sat, otherwise breezy but pleasant \nwith highs just a few degrees below normal.\n\nAn abnormally deep low over eastern Canada this evening will\nkeep a gusty northwest flow of air over central PA into Sat.\nNW wind gusts likely in the 20-30 mph range.\n\nA weak trough of low pressure dropping southeast on Saturday\nwill result in a chance of a shower or storm again.\n\nMidlevel lapse rates are steep enough for a few rumbles of \nthunder in the tallest showers (higher risk as you go north). \n\nTemperatures on Saturday will to be just a few degrees below \nnormal with plenty of diurnal cumulus.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKey Message 2: After a dry Sunday, more widespread rain and a \nchance of a thunderstorms return to the forecast for Sunday \nnight and Monday.\n\nWinds will diminish Saturday night and skies will range from\nmostly clear to partly cloudy. If skies are clear enough, there\ncould be some patchy fog by Sunday morning. After a slightly \nwarmer day on Sunday with lighter wind and a fair amount of \nsunshine, more widespread rainfall is likely Sunday night and \nMonday as a wave of low pressure lifts NE from the Lower Ohio \nRiver Valley.\n\nLatest model consensus is for between 0.75 and 1.50\" of rain \nwith this system. However, given the relatively small size of \nthis disturbance, we'll have to keep a close eye on bust \npotential if the track changes and brings the heaviest rain \nelsewhere.\n\nNew...\n\nA bit of model spread on the track of the sfc low. Also the 850\nmb low track is well north of the the sfc low track. Pattern is\none that can support heavy rain to the north and east of the \nlow. \n\nEarlier...\n\nWith high pressure building in behind the departing low, Tue and\nWed are likely to be on the dry side. Longer range guidance\npoints towards a potentially active end to the week as multiple\ndisturbances ride along a baroclinic zone bisecting the Mid \nAtlantic.",
